From: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Subject: nilfs2: report mapping errors from fsync()
Date: Sun, 22 Nov 2026 17:23:26 -0800

nilfs_sync_file() constructs a segment and flushes the device, but never
looks at the error state of the file's mapping.  Nothing else in nilfs2
does either.  So an error which was recorded with mapping_set_error() is
never reported to the application.

memory_failure() records -EIO that way when it has to discard a dirty
page-cache page.  On other filesystems the next fsync() then fails with
EIO, which is how the application learns that data it wrote has been
lost.  On nilfs2 fsync() returns 0.  The memory-failure selftest, once it
checks for this, fails on nilfs2 in 20 of 20 runs:

  memory-failure.c:413:dirty_pagecache:Expected fsync(fd) (0) == -1 (-1)

Call file_check_and_advance_wb_err() in nilfs_sync_file().  Call it even
when segment construction or the flush has failed, so that the recorded
error is consumed for this file either way, and return it if nothing
failed earlier.

Signed-off-by: Andrew Morton <akpm@linux-foundation.org>
Cc: Ryusuke Konishi <konishi.ryusuke@gmail.com>
Cc: Viacheslav Dubeyko <slava@dubeyko.com>
Cc: Miaohe Lin <linmiaohe@huawei.com>
Cc: Naoya Horiguchi <nao.horiguchi@gmail.com>
---
INSTRUCTIONS FOR THE TESTING CHAT.  Pass on everything from this line down
to the diffstat.

Goal: show that fsync() on nilfs2 now reports the -EIO which
memory_failure() records, and that ordinary fsync() is unaffected.

This patch changes the kernel.  Build TWO kernels from the same tree and
the same .config: K0 without this patch, K1 with it.  Use the config from
the earlier memory-failure run (NILFS2_FS=y, MEMORY_FAILURE,
HWPOISON_INJECT, DEBUG_FS, PROC_PAGE_MONITOR, BLK_DEV_LOOP, PROVE_LOCKING,
DEBUG_VM, DEBUG_LIST).  ext4 is the control throughout.

Test binaries, both built from a tree with
selftests-mm-check-that-fsync-reports-hard-poisoned-dirty-page-cache
applied: B as it is, and B-notrunc with its one ftruncate(fd, 0) line and
the comment above it deleted.

Part 1, on K0 then K1, cwd on a fresh nilfs2 loop mount (1G image), then
on ext4:

- B, 20 runs.  Expected on nilfs2: K0 0 of 20 (fsync() returned 0, then
  unpoison -EBUSY in teardown); K1 20 of 20.
- B-notrunc, 20 runs.  nilfs2 has no ->error_remove_folio(), so on K1 the
  fsync() assertion should now pass but teardown may still fail with

    memory-failure.c:211:...:Expected unpoison_memory(self->pfn) (-16) == 0

  Report how many of 20.  That failure is a known, separate problem and
  is not a regression from this patch.

Record for each: passes, every distinct failing assertion with its count,
the "recovery action for dirty LRU page" counts, and HardwareCorrupted in
/proc/meminfo before and after.

Part 2, on K1, nilfs2: the error is reported once and only once.  Write a
small program: create a file, ftruncate to one page, mmap MAP_SHARED,
write to the page, madvise(MADV_HWPOISON) with a SIGBUS handler
installed, munmap, then call fsync() three times and close().  Print
every return value and errno.  Expected: first fsync() -1/EIO, second and
third 0, close() 0.  Then repeat with a second file descriptor which was
open on the same file before the poisoning: each descriptor's first
fsync() should return -1/EIO.  Run the same program on ext4 and report
both side by side; any difference between nilfs2 on K1 and ext4 is a
finding.

Part 3, on K0 then K1, nilfs2: regressions.

- fsync() of a file with no error must still return 0: write 1000 files
  of 1 to 64 kB, fsync() and fdatasync() each, check every return value,
  unmount, remount, verify the contents.
- Whatever nilfs2 coverage the machine has, in this order of preference:
  fstests "-g quick" with FSTYP=nilfs2, otherwise fsx and fsstress for 10
  minutes each.  Report the failing tests on K0 and on K1.  Only
  differences between the two matter.
- Run nilfs-tools' checks if present (lscp, lssu) after each part and
  report anything they complain about.

Rules:

- A failure is a result.  Report it.  Do not change the kernel, the tests
  or the mount options to make something pass, other than making
  B-notrunc as described; say in the report that you did that.
- If something cannot be built, made or mounted, report it as NOT RUN
  with the exact command and error.
- Quote in full any WARNING, BUG, lockdep report or nilfs2 error message
  in the kernel log on K1 which is not also on K0.

Report: a K0/K1 table for Part 1, the return values for Part 2 with ext4
beside nilfs2, the two lists for Part 3, then anything NOT RUN.

 fs/nilfs2/file.c |   11 ++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/fs/nilfs2/file.c~nilfs2-report-mapping-errors-from-fsync
+++ a/fs/nilfs2/file.c
@@ -29,7 +29,7 @@ int nilfs_sync_file(struct file *file, l
 	 */
 	struct the_nilfs *nilfs;
 	struct inode *inode = file->f_mapping->host;
-	int err = 0;
+	int err = 0, wb_err;
 
 	if (nilfs_inode_dirty(inode)) {
 		if (datasync)
@@ -43,6 +43,15 @@ int nilfs_sync_file(struct file *file, l
 	if (!err)
 		err = nilfs_flush_device(nilfs);
 
+	/*
+	 * Report an error which was recorded in the mapping, for example by
+	 * memory_failure() on a dirty page.  Check even if the above failed,
+	 * so that the error is consumed for this file.
+	 */
+	wb_err = file_check_and_advance_wb_err(file);
+	if (!err)
+		err = wb_err;
+
 	return err;
 }
 
_
